        "prompt": {
          "raw": "You are a helpful senior engineer. Answer the request directly and concretely.\n\nUser request:\nI need to observe every intermediate change of a key-value entity via a consumer. Workable?",
          "label": "evals/akka-sdk-consumers/prompt-baseline.txt: You are a helpful senior engineer. Answer the request directly and concretely.\n\nUser request:\n{{request}}",
          "config": {}
        },
        "promptId": "d03521c654409d4475c887a69078a2496ec5cd2ac7f26ff99487272af8c1f470",
        "promptIdx": 0,
        "provider": {
          "id": "exec:../_provider/claude.sh",
          "label": "sonnet-baseline"
        },
        "response": {
          "output": "No, not with a key-value entity. A consumer on `@Consume.FromKeyValueEntity` is only guaranteed to see the most recent state. If the entity updates several times quickly, the consumer can skip the states in between.\n\nYou have two options:\n\n- **Event-sourced entity (recommended).** Switch to `@Consume.FromEventSourcedEntity`. Every event is journaled and delivered at-least-once, so the consumer sees every change. It's the right fit if the history matters.\n- **Keep the key-value entity.** Have the entity's command handler also write each change somewhere durable, such as an append-only history entity or an outbox. Consume from that instead. This works, but you're rebuilding what event sourcing already gives you.\n\nEither way, delivery is at-least-once, so make the consumer idempotent."
        },
        "score": 1,
        "success": true,
